{
  "id": "18751_sugoku",
  "headword": "すごく",
  "reading": "すごく",
  "romaji": "sugoku",
  "part_of_speech": "adverb",
  "gloss": "very; extremely; incredibly",
  "definitions": [
    {
      "sense_number": 1,
      "gloss": "very; extremely; incredibly; awfully",
      "explanation": "An intensifier used to emphasize the degree of an adjective or verb. Widely used in casual spoken Japanese. Originally the adverbial form of the adjective すごい, but now functions independently as an adverb."
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "18751_sugoku_ex1",
      "japanese": "すごく{楽|たの}しかった。",
      "english": "It was really fun.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "18751_sugoku_ex2",
      "japanese": "{今日|きょう}はすごく{寒|さむ}いね。",
      "english": "It's extremely cold today, isn't it.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    },
    {
      "id": "18751_sugoku_ex3",
      "japanese": "すごく{頑張|がんば}ったのに{結果|けっか}が{出|で}なかった。",
      "english": "I tried incredibly hard, but didn't get results.",
      "sense_numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "has_audio": false,
      "notes": null
    }
  ],
  "notes": "The standard adverbial form of すごい (amazing/terrible), used as an intensifier meaning \"very\" or \"extremely.\"\n\nUSAGE NOTE:\nIn casual speech, すごい is also widely used as an adverb (すごい{楽|たの}しい instead of すごく{楽|たの}しい). This is grammatically nonstandard but extremely common in everyday conversation.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS (intensifiers by register):\n- めちゃくちゃ / めっちゃ: super, crazy (very casual, young speakers)\n- すごく: very, extremely (casual)\n- とても: very (neutral, standard)\n- たいへん: very (slightly formal)\n- {非常|ひじょう}に: extremely (formal, written)\n\nREGISTER:\nすごく is informal and used primarily in spoken language. In formal writing, prefer とても or {非常|ひじょう}に.",
